---
import { Prism as Code } from '@astrojs/prism';
import Page from '../../layouts/Page.astro';
import '../../prism.ts';
// The demo is a machine with a view, used as a component: rendered to HTML
// when the site is built and taken over in the browser (@lucy/astro).
import { Dbmon } from '../../demos/dbmon/dbmon.lucy';
import dbmonLucy from '../../demos/dbmon/dbmon.lucy?raw';
import envJs from '../../demos/dbmon/env.js?raw';

const frontmatter = {
  title: 'DBMon',
  lede: 'The classic rendering benchmark: a table of databases and their slowest queries, changing as fast as the browser can draw.',
};
---
<Page frontmatter={frontmatter} styles={['demos']}>
  <p>
    <a href="https://github.com/mathieuancelin/js-repaint-perfs">DBMon</a> shows a pseudo-database
    monitor: a hundred databases, each with how many queries it’s running and its five slowest.
    On every animation frame, some share of them change: the slider sets how many.
    Hover a query to see it.
  </p>

  <figure class="demo demo--wide">
    <Dbmon client:load />
  </figure>

  <p>
    There are 601 machines: the page, a hundred databases and five hundred queries. Each one is
    sent a <code>frame</code> event, and a guard decides whether it takes a new sample. A sample
    is a few assigns, and each assign changes the one text node or attribute that shows its key.
    Nothing is compared, and nothing outside the changed cells is touched.
  </p>

  <h2 id="source">The source</h2>

  <p class="demo__file">dbmon.lucy</p>
  <Code lang="lucy" code={dbmonLucy.trimEnd()} />

  <p>
    The lists are filled by a state that goes back to itself: while the guard says there are more
    to make, its immediate transition appends one and enters the state again. Lucy’s views have
    no loops, and this keeps the counting in the machine.
  </p>

  <p class="demo__file">env.js</p>
  <Code lang="js" code={envJs.trimEnd()} />

  <h2 id="differences">How it differs from the original</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The original generates every database’s data in one place and renders the whole table
      from it. Here each database and each query is a machine that samples itself, so an update
      is local to the machine that changed.
    </li>
    <li>
      The original takes each database’s five slowest from a dozen queries. Here each of the five
      cells samples its own query.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p><a href="demos/">All demos</a></p>
</Page>
